{
  "title": "Serializer message-shape and PUBLISH recipient-filter integrity",
  "date": "2026-09-12",
  "finding": "SA-015",
  "threat_model": [
    "A peer can send a recognized WAMP message with fewer required fields and trigger serializer-specific indexing or cast failures before the router can normalize the protocol error.",
    "A publisher can supply scalar, mixed, fractional, zero, negative, or out-of-range recipient filters; permissive coercion can silently broaden or change the recipient set.",
    "Attacker-controlled filter values must not appear in diagnostics."
  ],
  "fix": {
    "message_shape": "JSON, MessagePack, and CBOR reject every recognized supported message family below its WAMP minimum field count with the constant FormatException message 'WAMP message contains too few fields'. Empty or non-integer message type slots are rejected without payload disclosure.",
    "numeric_filters": "PUBLISH.Options.exclude and eligible now require lists of integral WAMP IDs in the inclusive range [1, 2^53]. BigInt is accepted only after the same bounds check. A value-integrality check closes dart2js numeric type erasure for fractional values.",
    "identity_filters": "PUBLISH.Options exclude_authid, exclude_authrole, eligible_authid, and eligible_authrole now require lists containing only strings; values are no longer stringified.",
    "hot_paths": "JSON and MessagePack test empty PUBLISH option maps before normalization/copying. JSON returns the exact two-field UNSUBSCRIBED form directly, and MessagePack performs minimum checks inside already-matched branches. Non-empty PUBLISH option construction is outlined from the empty hot path.",
    "compatibility": "WAMP message codes, serializer wire formats, the inclusive WAMP ID upper bound, supported optional fields, and valid PUBLISH filter values are unchanged."
  },
